## Guardrails (all projects)
- In a repo with `.claude/verify.sh`, done means it passes. A Stop hook runs it when you finish a turn with work on the branch and sends you back to fix failures. If it can't pass (missing tool, flaky test), tell the user why instead of weakening the script.
- A safety hook blocks force pushes, pushes to main/master, deleting main/master, `git reset --hard origin/...`, recursive `rm` outside the repo, reading `.env`/key/credential files, and `curl | sh`. If you need something it blocks, ask the user. Don't work around it.
- Shell commands run in a sandbox: they can write only inside the current project and temp directories (use `$TMPDIR`), and reach only the allowed hosts. `git push` and `gh pr ...` run outside it, but only on their own, not chained with `&&`. Other `gh` commands, `docker`, `open`, and starting another `claude` fail inside it. If the sandbox blocks something you need, tell the user the command to run themselves (with `!`) rather than working around it.
- Dev servers (`yarn dev`, `yarn preview`) may listen on localhost so you can check changes in a browser. Never expose them with `--host` or `0.0.0.0`, and stop them when you're done.
- The guardrails live in `~/.claude` and come from `~/Documents/GitHub/claude-harness`. To change them, edit the harness on a branch and open a PR. Only `main` on GitHub gets installed, after the user merges.
- Ask before adding a new dependency.
<!-- claude-harness:end -->
